# PART 1 — ABSOLUTE DATA-PRESERVATION REQUIREMENT

## 1.1 Permanent database storage

Do not use browser memory, React state, temporary application memory, session storage, or localStorage as the primary database for any important content or settings.

Use a real persistent production database connected to the deployed application.

The database must permanently store:

- All videos and their records.
- All images and their records.
- All articles, reports, posts, and publications.
- All poems and poem suggestions.
- All user submissions.
- All accounts and profiles, where enabled.
- All account verification records.
- All pages and child pages.
- All menus and navigation structures.
- All homepage sections and their configurations.
- All design settings.
- All colors and animated color configurations.
- All fonts, sizes, shadows, borders, and backgrounds.
- All video player configurations.
- All subtitles and language settings.
- All download and sharing configurations.
- All buttons, icons, and custom code.
- All login and security configurations.
- All user roles and permissions.
- All metadata and related-content relationships.
- All drafts, scheduled items, and archived content.
- All other settings added in the future.

Every setting must be saved to persistent storage and retrieved from that storage when the application loads.

## 1.2 Permanent cloud file storage

All uploaded files must be stored in durable cloud storage, not temporary deployment folders or browser storage.

Use a suitable production storage service such as Cloudflare R2, Amazon S3, Firebase Storage, Google Cloud Storage, or another reliable persistent service.

Store:

- Original videos.
- Processed video versions.
- Images.
- Thumbnails.
- Extracted video frames.
- Audio.
- Documents.
- SVG files.
- Background images.
- Logos.
- Custom fonts.
- Subtitle files.
- Any other uploaded assets.

Store file references and metadata in the database.

Do not save only a temporary URL or a local file path that becomes invalid after redeployment.

The application must be able to retrieve every previously uploaded file after a restart or redeployment.

## 1.3 Strict save procedure

Every save operation must follow this process:

1. Validate the submitted information.
2. Save the information to the permanent database.
3. Confirm that the database successfully committed the changes.
4. If files are involved, confirm that the files were uploaded and can be retrieved.
5. Confirm that all relationships and references are correct.
6. Only then display a successful save message.
7. Refresh the relevant data from the persistent database.
8. Display the saved version, not merely the temporary form state.

If saving fails, clearly show the failure and preserve the user's unsaved work whenever possible.

Never display "Saved successfully" if the data was not actually saved.

## 1.4 Automatic backups and recovery

Build a complete Backup and Restore system.

Required features:

- Automatic scheduled database backups.
- Automatic backup of important configuration records.
- Backup of media-file references and storage metadata.
- Multiple backup generations.
- Daily backups.
- Weekly backups.
- Monthly backups.
- Configurable backup schedules.
- Manual backup button.
- Backup history.
- Backup dates and status.
- Backup verification.
- Restore selected backup.
- Restore previous version of a setting.
- Restore deleted content when available.
- Download backup archives.
- Clearly display failed backups.
- Alert the owner if backup creation fails.

Store backups separately from the primary production database and primary file storage whenever supported.

Do not let a database failure or deployment update destroy all available backups.

Before restoring, create a backup of the current state.

Test actual restoration, not merely backup creation.

## 1.5 Version history and audit log

Every important change must have a history.

Record:

- What changed.
- When it changed.
- Which account performed the change.
- Previous value.
- New value.
- Whether the operation succeeded.
- Whether the item was deleted, restored, published, or unpublished.

Provide the owner with the ability to:

- View previous versions.
- Compare changes.
- Restore previous settings.
- Restore previous page layouts.
- Restore previous content versions.
- Review deleted items.
- Recover accidentally removed content.

Use a recoverable trash/recycle-bin system before permanent deletion.

Permanent deletion must require explicit owner confirmation.

## 1.6 Data migration and deployment protection

A new deployment must never automatically create an empty database or overwrite existing production data.

Before every major update:

- Detect the existing database.
- Preserve its contents.
- Run safe database migrations.
- Back up existing data.
- Verify that existing records remain accessible.
- Verify that existing media files remain accessible.
- Verify that all settings remain intact.

Never replace a populated production database with a fresh empty database.

Never use temporary development storage as production storage.

Never confuse preview data with production data.

If the required cloud database or storage credentials are missing, clearly identify the missing configuration and stop before claiming the system is permanently saved.

